programmers-screenshot (0.26.0) noble; urgency=medium

  * A browser tab is now a two-call job from Claude in Chrome. The skill and
    --recipe-help name the handoff exactly -- one browser_batch that reads
    window.innerWidth and the rectangles and saves the screenshot to disk,
    then one run of --input -- and say the picture must never go through a
    nested claude --chrome -p session, which is where an hour went.
  * --viewport WIDTH (or "viewport" in a recipe) takes window.innerWidth and
    works the scale out from the picture's own width, so nothing has to open
    the file to measure it. --scale still wins when both are given.
  * --input is quiet: no shutter, no notification and no five-minute
    notification agent, because no screen was read. The path is still
    printed, and the clipboard is still yours to opt out of.

 -- Shubshub  Thu, 03 Sep 2026 12:24:08 +1200

programmers-screenshot (0.25.0) noble; urgency=medium

  * A screenshot can now be described rather than taken by hand, so that
    something other than a person can produce one. --recipe takes JSON naming
    a region, a delay, an output path and a list of marks: boxes, ellipses,
    lines, arrows, numbered step badges, labels, redaction bars and
    pixelation, in the colours and sizes the toolbar already offers. Nothing
    is drawn until the whole recipe has been understood, so a mistake in the
    last arrow does not leave the first three baked into a half-finished
    image; the message names the entry and the exit status is 2.
  * --recipe-help prints the whole reference, generated from the parser's own
    table so it cannot go stale, and needs no display.
  * --region X,Y,W,H captures an area with no overlay, and --delay N waits
    before the screen is read, so a menu or a hover state can be set up first.
    The delay works with the overlay too.
  * --window TITLE captures one window whatever is stacked on top of it.
    Nothing is raised and nothing is focused: under a compositor every window
    is drawn to an offscreen pixmap of its own, so a completely buried window
    can be photographed without disturbing it, and whatever is lying over it
    stays out of the picture. --list-windows says what can be named. X11 only,
    and it wants libwnck.
  * --input FILE annotates a picture something else took, with --scale for the
    size it came out at and --origin to move where coordinates start. This is
    how a browser tab is done: nothing outside a browser can address a tab, so
    the browser captures the tab it means and this annotates the result, with
    every coordinate the page's own.
  * --install-skill writes a Claude Code skill, so that a session in any
    project knows the command exists and how to drive it. --uninstall-skill
    takes it back.
  * All of it is off until switched on, in the settings window, for the same
    reason the update check is: a person pressing Print Screen knows what is
    on their screen and a script does not. Annotating a picture with --input
    is exempt, since it reads no screen at all.
  * Fix: a stray copy of the file list had been pasted into the middle of the
    --install-hotkey paragraph of the man page, splitting a sentence in two.

 -- Shubshub  Wed, 02 Sep 2026 14:20:00 +1200

